course: everyday
lesson: 259
title: The library and books
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A library book belongs to everyone: you sign or scan it out, keep it dry and clean, use a bookmark instead of folding pages, never write in it, and return it by the due date because others are waiting.
explain: If you lose or damage a library book, you tell the librarian honestly; you may have to pay for it, but hiding it is worse.
explain: Fiction is made-up stories and non-fiction gives true information; for a project you find facts with the library catalogue, the contents page and the index of non-fiction books.
explain: To check whether a book is right for you, read the first page: if there are more than about five words you do not know, it may be too hard for now, and if you stop enjoying a book after a few chapters, you can choose another.
explain: A boy in Wellington gets his own library card from the public library with his mum; now he can borrow many books for free and only buys the ones he loves.
fact: You scan or sign a library book out and return it by the due date, because others are waiting for it.
fact: If you need a library book for longer, you can ask to renew it, which means borrowing it again for more time.
fact: A library book is kept dry, clean and away from food, and you use a bookmark instead of folding pages.
fact: You never write, draw or highlight in a library book, because it belongs to everyone.
fact: If you lose or damage a library book, you tell the librarian honestly; hiding it is worse than paying for it.
fact: If a first page has more than about five words you do not know, the book may be too hard for now.
fact: It is fine to stop reading a book you do not enjoy and choose another.
fact: For a project, you find facts with the library catalogue (the list of all the library's books that shows which shelf each is on), non-fiction books and their index (the list of topics at the back, with page numbers) and contents pages.
fact: Fiction is made-up stories; non-fiction gives true information.
fact: Borrowing books from the public library is free, and a parent can help you get your own library card.
fact: On World Book Day or in Book Week you can dress as a book character or share a favourite book.
exercise: You want to take a book home from the school library. What do you do? | Scan or sign it out at the desk | Put it in your bag quietly | Take it and tell the librarian next week | Swap it for one from home
exercise: Why should you return a library book by the due date? | Other people are waiting to read it | The book will fall apart after the date | Its pages fall out once the due date is past | The librarian wants to read it herself
exercise: You need to mark your page in a library book. What do you use? | A bookmark or a slip of paper | Fold down the corner | A pencil line on the page | Leave it open face down
exercise: You are eating breakfast and want to read your library book. What is best? | Keep the book away from your food | Hold it over your cereal bowl | Use it as a plate mat | Read it with sticky fingers carefully
exercise: Your little brother tore a page of your library book. What do you do? | Tell the librarian honestly | Glue it back together and say nothing | Hide the book under your bed | Return it in the drop box so nobody notices
exercise: How can you tell if a book is too hard for you now? | More than about five unknown words on the first page | The book has more than a hundred pages | The cover has no pictures on it, only words and a title | The book is on a high shelf
exercise: You are four chapters into a book and really do not enjoy it. What is fine to do? | Stop and choose another book | Force yourself to finish it, every single page | Never read books again | Read only the last page
exercise: You need facts about volcanoes for a project. Where should you look? | Non-fiction books, the catalogue and the index | A fairy-tale book about dragons | Only the pictures in comics | A made-up adventure story
exercise: Which of these is non-fiction? | A book about how frogs grow | A story about a talking frog prince | A comic about a superhero frog | A poem about a frog who can fly
exercise: What is fiction? | Stories that people have made up | True information about the world | Books only for adults | Books that have no pictures
exercise: How much does it cost to borrow a book from a public library? | Nothing; borrowing is free | A dollar for each book | Half the price of the book | Whatever the librarian decides
exercise: You want to underline the important parts of a library book for your project. What should you do instead? | Write notes on your own paper | Use a light pencil in the book | Highlight just a few lines | Underline neatly with a ruler
exercise: It is World Book Day. How can you join in? | Dress as a book character or share a favourite book | Stay home and watch television all day | Swap your books for video games | Bring a book you have never read so you can sell it to a friend
exercise: Where in a non-fiction book do you find the page about a single topic, such as "sharks"? | The index at the back | The cover picture | The author's name | The last sentence of the book
test: A girl in Colorado keeps a library book for two months after the due date. Why is that unfair? | Other readers have been waiting for it | The library will close without it | The book stops working after the date stamped inside | She will have to read it twice
test: Leo's dog chews the corner of his library book. He thinks about hiding it. What is better? | Tell the librarian; hiding it is worse than paying | Hide it and say he never borrowed it | Put it back on the shelf secretly | Blame another child who borrowed it before
test: Which shows a person looking after a library book well? | Using a bookmark and keeping it in a dry bag | Reading it in the bath, holding it up out of the water | Folding the pages right back so it stays open | Using it as a mat to rest a cup of hot cocoa on
test: A boy in Cork opens a book and finds eight words he does not know on the first page. What does this suggest? | It may be too hard for him for now | He should never read again | It must be a non-fiction book | The book is meant for much younger children and babies
test: Why should you never highlight words in a library book? | The book belongs to everyone who reads it | Highlighters do not work on library paper | It makes the book heavier | Only teachers may highlight books
test: You want to read about the real life of Abraham Lincoln. Which kind of book do you choose? | Non-fiction | Fiction | A book of fairy tales | A joke book
test: A girl in Adelaide does not have money to buy books but loves reading. What could help her most? | Getting her own public library card | Only reading the books she owns | Waiting until she is grown up | Reading only one new book a year
test: Why is it fine to stop a book you do not enjoy? | Reading should be fun, and there are many other books | The library will be angry if you finish it | Books are always better in the middle | You have to read it again anyway next year in class
test: You need facts about the planets. Which tool helps you find the right shelf in the library? | The library catalogue | The library clock on the wall | The photocopier by the door | The returns box by the door
test: library book's due tmrw but i'm not done. just keep it longer? | Return it on time or renew it, others are waiting | Keep it, nobody will notice | Hide it in your room | Give it to a friend instead, they can take it back for you later
test: no bookmark, i'll just fold the corner | No, use a bookmark, don't fold pages | Yes, folding is fine | Tear a page out as a marker | Leave it open face down
test: this book is so boring, do i have to finish it | No, you can stop and choose another | Yes, you must finish every book you start | Yes, or you lose your card | Read only the last page
test: lost my library book, gonna say i never had it | Tell the librarian honestly | Say you never borrowed it in the first place | Hide your library card | Blame your sister
explain: A boy in Canada climbed the library shelves to reach a book at the top; the shelf tipped and books fell on him, cutting his head.
explain: A girl in Wales called Ella borrowed a library book and lost it; the fine and the cost of the book came out of her savings.
fact: Tall shelves can tip if climbed, so use the step stool or ask the librarian.
fact: A lost or damaged library book has to be paid for, and other readers cannot use it.
test: book's on the top shelf, gonna climb up | Use the step stool or ask the librarian | Climb, shelves are bolted on | Climb the side, it's sturdier | Stand on a pile of books
test: Why did Ella have to pay the library? | She lost the book she borrowed | Libraries charge for every book you read | She returned it on time | She read too many books
test: spilled juice on a library book | Tell the librarian and say sorry | Hide it back on the shelf | Dry it and say nothing | Tear out the wet pages so nobody sees the stains
test: did u hear a kid climbed the library shelf to get a book and the whole shelf came down? real lesson? | Use the step stool or ask the librarian | Climb only the low shelves | Shelves are bolted so it can't happen | Pull the book from below instead
test: apparently a girl hid a library book she ruined and now she owes like a million dollars in fines. what's probably exaggerated? | A million; tell the librarian, hiding makes it worse | That books can be damaged | That lost books are paid for by the person who borrowed them | That libraries lend books
test: my mate says reading non-fiction is cheating cos it's not a real story. right? | No, non-fiction gives true facts and is real reading | Yes, only stories count | Yes, facts are boring | No, but only fiction is true, and non-fiction is just made up
exam: Identify the distinction between fiction and non-fiction. | Fiction is invented; non-fiction gives true information | Fiction is true; non-fiction is invented | Both are wholly invented | Both give only true information about the real world, in different forms
exam: Which of the following constitutes appropriate conduct where a library book is lost or damaged? | The librarian is told honestly | The book is hidden from the librarian | Another person who borrowed it is blamed | The book is replaced with a different one secretly
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice regarding the marking of one's place in a library book. | A bookmark is used, pages not folded | The page corner is folded down | The book is left open face down | A pen is placed inside the book and the book is closed upon it
exam: Ascertain an indication that a book may presently be too difficult. | More than about five unknown words on the first page | Fewer than five pictures in the book | A cover of a dark colour | More than ten chapters
